Write a development certificate authority and server certificate, so
`nexora observe` can serve HTTPS on this machine.

  Files written to --out
  ──────────────────────
    ca.pem          The certificate authority to trust: give it to curl
                    (--cacert), the Nexora CLI (--cacert) and your browser.
    server.pem      The server certificate, for --tls-cert.
    server-key.pem  Its private key, for --tls-key. Readable only by you.

  The server certificate is valid for localhost, 127.0.0.1 and ::1, plus every
  --host, for --days days (default 365, at most 825). The CA's own key is not
  kept, so nothing else can ever be signed with a CA you have trusted. Run the
  command again for a new set; existing files are kept unless --force is given.

  These certificates are for development. In production use one from your CA
  or Let's Encrypt.

  Exit codes
  ──────────
  0  Certificates written
  1  The files could not be written
  2  Invalid arguments, or the files already exist without --force

  Examples
  ────────
  Certificates for localhost:
    nexora tls-gen

  Also valid for a LAN name and address:
    nexora tls-gen --out ./certs --host nexora.local --host 192.168.1.20

  Serve and call it:
    nexora observe --tls-cert ./certs/server.pem --tls-key ./certs/server-key.pem
    curl --cacert ./certs/ca.pem https://localhost:9464/health/live
